(*
rete#162 — the node's per-boot identity. `bootId` rode the pull and push envelopes
as the source constant 1 from the space-sync rung until 2026-08-09, and that
constant is what cost c6-bell a night of telemetry: porta cannot tell "the node
rebooted, so its seq counter restarted" from "porta's ack went missing, so the node
is re-offering rows it already sent", and those two need opposite responses.

WHAT THIS TEST CAN AND CANNOT SEE. The real increment lives in NVS and only a power
cycle exercises it, so this asserts the two properties a host CAN prove and that a
board cannot prove cheaply:

  1. It is a NUMBER the VM answers, not a literal the image carries. A regression
     here is the seam being unwired, which is exactly how it would silently revert
     to a constant.
  2. It is CONSTANT WITHIN A BOOT. This is correctness, not thrift: porta uses a
     CHANGE in this value as its sole evidence that the outbound sequence restarted,
     so two different answers in one run would rewind porta's high-water mid-session
     and re-deliver rows it had already committed.

With TUVM_BOOTID_FILE unset — the default everywhere in this suite — the host
backend answers the 0 sentinel, meaning "no persistent boot identity". That is a
value to branch on and not an error: porta reads it as "restart detection is
unavailable for this node" and says so on every check-in, which is the one thing
the old constant could never do.
expect:
is integer true
constant within a boot true
sentinel when unpersisted 0
*)
class BootId packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | a b |
    a:: platform bootId.
    b:: platform bootId.
    ('is integer ', (a isNil not and: [ (a + 0) = a ]) printString) println.
    ('constant within a boot ', (a = b) printString) println.
    ('sentinel when unpersisted ', a printString) println.
    ^ self )
)
